(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-lung-thorax-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Lung & Thorax Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The angle of Louis (sternal angle) is a useful anatomic landmark because it...","choices":["marks the 2nd rib/intercostal space and corresponds to the tracheal bifurcation and upper border of the atria","has no relationship whatsoever to counting ribs or locating any intercostal spaces at all","is located at the level of the umbilicus, well below the entire thoracic cavity itself","marks the 5th rib exclusively, with no relation to any deeper internal thoracic structures"],"answer":0,"exp":"The angle of Louis is continuous with the 2nd rib, providing a starting point to count ribs/intercostal spaces to localize findings; it also marks the level where the trachea bifurcates into the main bronchi and corresponds to the upper border of the cardiac atria. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"To locate the angle of Louis, the examiner starts at the sternal notch and...","choices":["walks the fingers down the manubrium a few centimeters until feeling a distinct bony ridge","presses directly over the xiphoid process at the very base of the sternum","moves laterally to the midclavicular line immediately, without further palpation","counts ribs starting from the 12th rib and working upward toward the sternum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Starting at the sternal notch, the examiner walks the fingers down the manubrium a few centimeters to feel the distinct bony ridge that is the sternal angle (angle of Louis). — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"When taking a review of systems related to a respiratory complaint, key elements to characterize a cough include its onset, nature, pattern, severity, and...","choices":["only their preferred pharmacy","only the patient's occupation","associated symptoms","only their family's insurance status"],"answer":2,"exp":"A thorough cough history characterizes onset, nature, pattern, severity, and any associated symptoms (for example, fever, weight loss, hemoptysis), which together help build the differential. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Among the listed risk factors for pulmonary disease, which grouping is accurate?","choices":["None of the listed factors have any meaningful effect whatsoever on pulmonary disease risk","Only consistently high levels of regular physical activity, with no other contributing factors","Only a young age and female gender, with no other listed contributing risk factors","Male gender, older age, smoking, sedentary lifestyle, occupational exposure, obesity, and recurrent chest infections"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture lists these specific risk factors for pulmonary disease: male gender (more than female), older age, smoking, sedentary lifestyle, occupational exposure, obesity, and a history of recurrent chest infections. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"On inspection of the thorax, the examiner should assess shape and symmetry, A/P diameter, and screen for...","choices":["scoliosis, kyphosis, tracheal displacement, and pectus deformities","only heart murmurs detected on cardiac auscultation exam","only the patient's measured resting blood pressure value","only bowel sounds heard during abdominal auscultation exam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thoracic inspection includes screening for spinal deformities (scoliosis, kyphosis), tracheal displacement, and chest wall deformities such as pectus excavatum or pectus carinatum. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Pectus excavatum, one of the chest wall deformities screened for on inspection, is best described as...","choices":["a normal variant present in nearly every patient","a protrusion or outward bulging of the sternum","a finding detected only through chest auscultation","a depression/sunken appearance of the sternum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pectus excavatum is a chest wall deformity in which the sternum appears sunken or depressed inward, in contrast to pectus carinatum, where the sternum protrudes outward. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"Assessing chest expansion (lung/respiratory excursion) is performed at approximately the level of the...","choices":["the 2nd rib exclusively, with the examiner's fingers pressed flat against the sternum","10th rib, with the examiner's fingers loosely grasping the rib cage while the patient inhales deeply","the clavicle exclusively, with the examiner's fingers hooked directly over the bone","the xiphoid process only, with no specific rib-level reference point being used"],"answer":1,"exp":"Chest expansion is assessed at about the level of the 10th rib, with the examiner's thumbs meeting near the spine and fingers loosely grasping the lateral rib cage; the patient inhales deeply while the examiner watches the distance the thumbs move apart. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"During chest expansion assessment, the examiner specifically watches for...","choices":["the patient's heart rate on the cardiac monitor, exclusively","changes in the patient's overlying skin color, exclusively","the size and reactivity of the patient's pupils, exclusively","the distance between the thumbs as the patient inhales deeply"],"answer":3,"exp":"As the patient takes a deep breath, the examiner watches the thumbs (positioned near the spine) move apart symmetrically; the distance they separate reflects the degree of chest expansion, and asymmetry can indicate unilateral lung pathology. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Vesicular breath sounds, one of the three normal breath sound types, are characterized by...","choices":["equal inspiratory and expiratory sounds, of intermediate intensity and pitch throughout","complete absence of any audible sound throughout the entire respiratory breathing cycle","expiratory sounds lasting longer than inspiratory sounds, loud and high-pitched throughout","inspiratory sounds lasting longer than expiratory sounds, soft in intensity and low in pitch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vesicular breath sounds — heard over most of the normal lung fields — have inspiratory sounds that last longer than expiratory sounds, and are soft in intensity and low in pitch. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Bronchovesicular breath sounds are characterized by...","choices":["a description with essentially no defined or reproducible acoustic characteristics at all","expiratory sounds much longer than inspiratory sounds, loud and high-pitched throughout","inspiratory sounds much longer than expiratory sounds, soft and low-pitched throughout","inspiratory and expiratory sounds that are roughly equal in length, of intermediate intensity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bronchovesicular breath sounds have inspiratory and expiratory phases that are approximately equal in duration and intermediate in intensity, typically heard over the main bronchi/central airways. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Bronchial (tracheal/tubular) breath sounds are characterized by...","choices":["a description that does not apply anywhere within a normal, healthy adult chest exam","equal inspiratory and expiratory sounds of intermediate intensity throughout the cycle","expiratory sounds lasting longer than inspiratory sounds, loud in intensity and high in pitch","inspiratory sounds lasting longer than expiratory sounds, soft in intensity and low in pitch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bronchial (tubular) breath sounds — normally heard only over the trachea/large airways — have expiratory sounds lasting longer than inspiratory sounds, and are loud and high-pitched; hearing this sound pattern over peripheral lung fields is abnormal and suggests consolidation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Adventitious breath sounds — abnormal sounds superimposed on the normal breath sounds — include rales (crackles), rhonchi, wheezes, and...","choices":["vesicular sounds","bronchial sounds heard appropriately over the trachea","bronchovesicular sounds","friction rubs"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adventitious breath sounds listed include rales (crackles), rhonchi, wheezes, and friction rubs (plus the less common amphoric sounds), all of which represent abnormal findings superimposed on the normal breath sound pattern. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"Bronchophony is assessed by having the patient say 'ninety-nine' while the examiner auscultates; a positive finding (louder, clearer transmission) suggests...","choices":["normal vesicular breath sounds","underlying lung consolidation","pneumothorax exclusively","a completely normal lung finding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Increased, clearer transmission of the spoken voice (bronchophony) occurs when underlying lung tissue is consolidated (for example, from pneumonia), since consolidated tissue transmits sound more efficiently than normal aerated lung. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"Egophony is assessed by having the patient say a prolonged 'E'; a positive finding is when the sound heard through the stethoscope changes to...","choices":["an 'A' sound (a nasal, goat-bleating quality)","exactly the same clear, unchanged 'E' sound","a clearly whispered 'ninety-nine' phrase instead","complete silence with no audible sound at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"In egophony, the 'E' sound the patient says is transmitted through the stethoscope as an 'A' sound with a nasal, bleating quality — classically heard over an area of consolidation or along the upper border of a pleural effusion. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"Whispered pectoriloquy is assessed by having the patient whisper '1-2-3' or 'ninety-nine'; a positive finding is when the whispered words are heard...","choices":["exactly as faint and muffled as normal, unchanged","only as a nasal, goat-bleating 'A' sound instead","loudly and with clearer articulation than expected","not at all audible, not even faintly through the stethoscope"],"answer":2,"exp":"In whispered pectoriloquy, the normally muffled whispered words are instead heard loudly and clearly through the stethoscope, an extreme form of increased voice transmission associated with consolidation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"To assess diaphragmatic excursion posteriorly, the examiner first asks the patient to exhale and hold it while percussing down the scapular line until the sound changes from resonant to dull, marking that spot, then...","choices":["moves directly to auscultation of the lung bases instead, entirely skipping this second percussion step","repeats the identical exhale-and-hold maneuver a second time at the exact same previously marked percussion level","asks the patient to take a deep breath and hold it, continuing to percuss down to the new level where the sound becomes dull, marking that spot too","immediately measures the total excursion distance without ever marking a second reference spot on the back"],"answer":2,"exp":"After marking the level at full exhalation, the examiner has the patient take a few normal breaths, then take a deep breath and hold it, continuing percussion downward to mark the new resonant-to-dull transition point — the distance between the two marks is the diaphragmatic excursion. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"Normal diaphragmatic excursion measures approximately how much bilaterally in adults, potentially up to 7-8 cm in well-conditioned individuals?","choices":["1 to 2 cm, with little variation across fitness levels","3 to 5 cm (up to 5.5 cm per the summary slide)","6 to 8 cm, expected even in an average, untrained adult","9 to 11 cm, typical bilaterally in most healthy adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal diaphragmatic excursion is approximately 3 to 5 cm (summarized elsewhere as up to 5.5 cm) in typical adults, and can be as much as 7-8 cm in well-conditioned individuals with greater respiratory reserve. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"The level of the diaphragm on percussion is often normally higher on the right side compared with the left because of the...","choices":["heart","spleen","liver","stomach"],"answer":2,"exp":"The liver, positioned beneath the right hemidiaphragm, pushes the diaphragm slightly higher on the right side compared with the left in many individuals, a normal asymmetry to keep in mind when assessing diaphragmatic level. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Decreased diaphragmatic excursion on assessment can result from several possible causes, including atelectasis of the lower lobes, emphysema, pleural effusion, pain, or...","choices":["well-controlled asthma with normal spirometry values","abdominal changes such as tumors or extreme ascites","vigorous aerobic exercise training performed three times weekly","normal aging alone, without any underlying lung disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Beyond pulmonary causes (atelectasis, emphysema, pleural effusion) and pain limiting inspiratory effort, decreased diaphragmatic excursion can also result from abdominal processes that restrict diaphragmatic descent, such as abdominal tumors or extreme ascites. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"When percussing the posterior chest, the examiner should proceed systematically, comparing side to side, at intervals of approximately...","choices":["from inferior to superior only, never the reverse","20 cm intervals","4 to 5 cm, moving from superior to inferior","only at a single fixed point, with no systematic movement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Posterior chest percussion is performed systematically, comparing side to side at each level, at roughly 4-5 cm intervals, proceeding from superior to inferior across the intercostal spaces. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"Increased voice transmission findings (bronchophony, whispered pectoriloquy) occur in situations where...","choices":["the lung is hyperinflated with chronic air trapping from emphysema","a large pneumothorax has caused collapse of the underlying lung tissue","normal vesicular breath sounds are heard throughout both lung fields","bronchial breathing is heard, making the sounds loud, sharp, and distinct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased voice transmission occurs any time bronchial-type breath sounds are present over an area that should normally have vesicular sounds, making transmitted voice sounds loud, sharp, and distinct rather than muffled — a sign of consolidation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"A quantitative decrease in voice transmission (decreased fremitus/resonance) could be due to...","choices":["normal, healthy lung tissue in a well-conditioned adult with no cardiopulmonary disease","any form of lung or pleural disease that impairs sound conduction, such as pleural effusion or pneumothorax","consolidation from lobar pneumonia exclusively, which instead increases transmission","egophony, a qualitative E-to-A voice change, occurring exclusively over consolidated lung"],"answer":1,"exp":"A quantitative decrease in voice transmission can result from any condition that impairs sound conduction from the lung to the chest wall, such as pleural effusion, pneumothorax, or other pleural/lung disease creating an air or fluid barrier. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for palpation of the chest are a midline trachea, no tenderness, and...","choices":["significant tenderness bilaterally","asymmetric, markedly increased fremitus on one side","a deviated trachea toward one side","symmetric, unaccentuated tactile fremitus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal palpation findings include a midline trachea, absence of tenderness, and tactile fremitus that is symmetric and not accentuated (increased) on either side. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 58"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for percussion of the chest are resonant, symmetric percussion notes and a diaphragmatic excursion range of...","choices":["15 to 20 cm","3 to 5 cm","dull, asymmetric percussion notes","0 cm (no measurable excursion)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The normal-findings summary specifies resonant, symmetric percussion notes throughout the lung fields and a diaphragmatic excursion in the 3-5 cm range (consistent with the earlier taught normal range). — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for auscultation of the chest are an absence of adventitious sounds and vesicular sounds throughout, except...","choices":["everywhere in the chest, with absolutely no exceptions to this pattern","over the abdomen, where bronchial breath sounds are normally heard","near the sternal borders, where bronchovesicular sounds are normally heard","at the lung apices exclusively, where tracheal sounds predominate"],"answer":2,"exp":"While most of the peripheral lung fields normally have vesicular breath sounds, bronchovesicular sounds are normally heard near the sternal borders/central airways, reflecting the transition zone near the larger airways. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"Tactile fremitus, assessed during chest palpation, refers to...","choices":["the patient's respiratory rate, counted by observing chest wall movement over a full sixty-second period","the palpable vibration felt on the chest wall as the patient speaks (for example, says 'ninety-nine')","a percussion note produced by striking the pleximeter finger with the plexor, heard without any palpation","a visual inspection finding, noted simply by observing the chest wall's movement and symmetry during breathing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tactile fremitus is the vibration felt by the examiner's hands on the chest wall as the patient speaks, reflecting how well the underlying lung tissue transmits vocal vibrations — increased over consolidation, decreased over effusion/pneumothorax/hyperinflation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"Besides tenderness and tactile fremitus, chest palpation also assesses for pulsations, bulges, depressions, crepitus, tracheal position, and...","choices":["symmetry of eye movements","displacement of mammary tissue","frequency of bowel sounds heard","pure-tone hearing thresholds"],"answer":1,"exp":"The palpation checklist specifically includes assessing for displacement of mammary tissue, alongside tenderness, pulsations, bulges, depressions, crepitus, respiratory excursion, tactile fremitus, and tracheal position. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"Direct versus indirect percussion, as distinguished in this lecture, differ primarily in that indirect percussion...","choices":["requires no side-to-side comparison, relying instead on a single strike per examined location","uses a pleximeter finger (placed on the chest) struck by the plexor finger of the other hand","involves striking the chest wall directly with one finger, without using a pleximeter at all","is performed only over the abdomen and is never used during the standard pulmonary exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Indirect percussion, the standard technique used clinically, involves placing the pleximeter (a finger of the non-dominant hand) firmly against the chest wall and striking it with the plexor (a finger of the dominant hand), in contrast to direct percussion, which strikes the chest wall directly. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"Screening tests referenced in the past medical history section for pulmonary conditions include skin testing, chest X-ray, and...","choices":["a resting echocardiogram","peripheral visual field testing","pulmonary function tests (PFTs)","a screening colonoscopy exam"],"answer":2,"exp":"The past medical history/testing section references skin testing (for example, for TB (tuberculosis)), CXR (chest x-ray), and pulmonary function tests (PFTs) as relevant prior workup to ask about. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for inspection of the chest are symmetry and...","choices":["absence of retraction or accessory muscle use","marked asymmetry of the chest wall on inspection","visible intercostal and suprasternal retractions","prominent use of accessory muscles at rest"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal inspection findings include chest wall symmetry and the absence of retractions or accessory muscle use during breathing, both of which would suggest increased work of breathing if present. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 58"}]};
